      <!-- SECTION 14: FAQ -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. Is MSDS mandatory for chemical products in India?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Yes. MSDS is mandatory under Indian hazardous chemical rules and the Factory Act wherever chemicals are manufactured, stored or handled. It is also required by transporters, warehouses and many institutional buyers for safety and compliance.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. Can a TDS replace an MSDS for chemical products?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              No. A TDS cannot replace an MSDS. TDS focuses on technical performance and application data, while MSDS is a legal safety document that covers hazards, emergency response and regulatory information.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. Is a COA required for every batch of production?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              Yes. A Certificate of Analysis is normally generated for every individual batch, especially in B2B, export, pharma, food-contact, automotive and regulated applications. It verifies that the batch meets agreed specifications.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0;">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Who can legally prepare an MSDS for a chemical product?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
              An MSDS should be prepared by a qualified chemical professional or a certified documentation service using GHS-compliant regulatory data. The manufacturer is ultimately responsible for the accuracy and completeness of the MSDS.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
